WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.709
Thousands of rows, but you only need the totals?

2
00:00:03.709 --> 00:00:05.972
Group By does it in one step.

3
00:00:05.972 --> 00:00:10.498
In this lesson, you will learn to group a table by a column, count

4
00:00:10.498 --> 00:00:14.700
the rows in each group, and get a clean summary table. Let's summarize!

5
00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:19.171
On the Transform tab, click Group By.

6
00:00:19.171 --> 00:00:21.375
Choose the column to group by.

7
00:00:21.375 --> 00:00:22.844
Here I choose Province.

8
00:00:22.844 --> 00:00:28.720
Then give the new column a name, Number of Surveys, and choose the operation: Count Rows.

9
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:30.859
Click OK.

10
00:00:30.859 --> 00:00:33.513
The whole table becomes a short summary:

11
00:00:33.513 --> 00:00:38.064
one row for each province, with the number of surveys done there.

12
00:00:38.064 --> 00:00:40.340
Perfect for checking your field progress!

13
00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:53.693
Let's try it on the Financial Sample.

14
00:00:53.693 --> 00:00:59.620
I group by Product, name the new column Most Product Sales, and again choose Count Rows.

15
00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:16.140
Now we see how many rows, or sales records, each product has.

16
00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:19.920
Other operations include Sum, Average, Median, Min, and Max.

17
00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:26.492
Remember:

18
00:01:26.492 --> 00:01:28.847
Group By replaces your detailed table.

19
00:01:28.847 --> 00:01:34.340
If you still need the details, duplicate the query first, and group the copy.

20
00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:48.792
Next, I open Enter data to build a small table for our next lesson

21
00:01:48.792 --> 00:01:50.133
on pivot and unpivot.

22
00:01:50.133 --> 00:01:55.160
Group By is one of the most useful buttons in Power Query. Use it often!

